{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Lesson 5</p>
  <h1>Routes</h1>
  <p class="lede">There is no router to learn. A request's path is a string; Roc's
  <code>match</code> picks the page.</p>
</header>

<section>
  <p>Try these links. They are not pages yet:
  <a class="pill" href="/greet/Ada">/greet/Ada</a>
  <a class="pill" href="/greet/Grace">/greet/Grace</a></p>
</section>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <p>In <code>main.roc</code>, find <code>page</code>. Each branch matches a path split on
  <code>/</code>. Where it says <em>a route of your own goes here</em>, add:</p>
  <pre><code>["", "greet", name] =&gt; Ok(Rocstache.html(Greeting.template({ frame: frame("/routes"), name })))</code></pre>
  <p>Save, then follow the links again. <code>name</code> is whatever the path holds there:
  a pattern binds it.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>How a request flows</h2>
  <ol class="flow">
    <li><code>init!</code> runs once, at start: it opens the database and returns the
    app's <code>Context</code>, shared by every request.</li>
    <li><code>respond!</code> runs for each request, on its own lightweight thread: the
    routes that read a file or the database are matched there.</li>
    <li>Everything else goes to <code>page</code>, a plain function: a path in, a response
    out. It renders nothing and reads nothing, so it is just data, which the next lesson
    tests.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <code>!</code> marks a function with effects. Roc tracks them in the types, so the
  pages that need none stay pure, and you can see which is which.</p>
</section>
